<%- include('layout-start.ejs', { title: 'Configuration — Accessories', basePath, activeNav: 'getting-started' }) %>
<div class="main-container">
    <div class="getting-started-layout">
        <%- include('sidebar-docs.ejs', { basePath, manualChapter: 2, configStep: 2 }) %>
        <div class="getting-started-layout-main">
            <div class="full-width-section">
                <h1>Accessories</h1>
                <p>Points, signals, crossings, and other layout items are configured on the <strong>Panels DCC website</strong>. Each accessory has a name, a type that looks like the real thing, and a DCC number/state for control.</p>
                <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= panelsAppOrigin %>/accessories" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open accessories on the Panels DCC website</a></p>
                <%- include('doc-screenshot-placeholder.ejs', { src: '/images/accessory-control-annotated.png', alt: 'Accessories in PanelsDCC Control', caption: 'Once saved, the same accessories appear in Control with live (best-effort) state.' }) %>

                <h2>Step 1: Choose a type</h2>
                <p>Click <strong>Add New Accessory</strong>. You first pick a type from the library of built in accessories, or you can also create a custom accessory.</p>
                <p>Some of the built in accessories are:</p>
                <div class="doc-icon-row">
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/point-rh-up.svg" alt="Point or turnout"/>
                        <span>Point / Turnout</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/two-aspect-home.svg" alt="Colour-light signal"/>
                        <span>Colour signal</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/four-aspect.svg" alt="Four-aspect colour signal"/>
                        <span>Four aspect</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/home-gwr.svg" alt="Semaphore signal"/>
                        <span>Semaphore</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/open-closed.svg" alt="Open or closed accessory"/>
                        <span>Open / closed</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/level-crossing.svg" alt="Level crossing"/>
                        <span>Level crossing</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="doc-icon-card">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/custom-accessory.svg" alt="Custom accessory"/>
                        <span>Custom</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>Once you pick the main type, e.g. semaphore signal or point or turnout, you can then pick a subtype if available. For example, a semaphore signal can be a home signal, and upper quadrant (LMS) and lower quadrant (GWR) types are avaialble. Likewise digital signals and points have different subtypes.</p>
                <%- include('doc-screenshot-placeholder.ejs', { label: 'Accessory type picker' }) %>

                <h2>Step 2: Name and picture</h2>
                <p>Give the accessory a short <strong>Name</strong> (required, up to 20 characters) — something you will recognise on a panel, such as “P1” or “Up home”.</p>

                <h2>Step 3: Options and DCC numbers</h2>
                <p>Having selected the accessory subtype, the required states will be shown and you can then enter the DCC number and state for each. (How those numbers and states work is covered under <a href="#dcc-table">DCC numbers and the audit table</a> below.)</p>
                <p>When configuring accessories for the first time, it is often easier to configure the accessory first in PanelsDCC and then programme the accessory on the layout using the accessories learning feature (if available).</p>
                <%- include('doc-screenshot-placeholder.ejs', { label: 'Accessory options table with DCC numbers' }) %>

                <h2>Step 4: Save and repeat</h2>
                <p>Click <strong>Create Accessory</strong>. Add the other points and signals you will place on a panel next. You can also create an accessory later from the panel editor if you realise something is missing — see <a href="<%= basePath %>/configuration/panels.html">Panels</a>.</p>
                <p>When you want one command to set several accessories together, see <a href="<%= basePath %>/configuration/macros.html">Macros</a>.</p>

                <h2 id="dcc-table">Managing lots of accessories</h2>
                <p>In DCC, an accessory is controlled by an <strong>accessory number</strong> and a <strong>state</strong>. That address space is separate from trains, so an accessory can reuse a number that a locomotive already has.</p>
                <p>Each accessory number has two states. Controllers may call them forward/reverse or open/closed; Panels DCC uses <strong>forward</strong> and <strong>reverse</strong>. When you configure an accessory, you map each visual state (normal/thrown, red/green, and so on) to a number and a direction.</p>
                <p><strong>Many accessories need more than one DCC number and this makes it confusing to manage and remember what is what.</strong> For example a three-aspect signal typically uses two DCC addresses, but only three of the four states (the first DCC address and two states for danger/red and caution/amber, and the second DCC address and one state for clear/green).</p>
                <p>To make it easier to manage accessories, PanelsDCC provides a <strong>Table View</strong> of the accessories and their DCC numbers and states. This allows you to audit which DCC numbers you have aassigned to what and spot any clashes.</p>
                <p><strong>Note:</strong> In many cases, to make things easier, you can point several accessories at the same number and state so one command flips many things. Panels DCC allows this, but it is clearer to give each accessory its own numbers and use <a href="<%= basePath %>/configuration/macros.html">macros</a> when you want one tap to move several items.</p>
                <%- include('doc-screenshot-placeholder.ejs', { label: 'Accessory Table View — DCC numbers used by what' }) %>

                <%- include('getting-started-pager.ejs', {
                    pagerPrevHref: basePath + '/configuration/functions.html',
                    pagerPrevTitle: 'Functions',
                    pagerNextHref: basePath + '/configuration/macros.html',
                    pagerNextTitle: 'Macros'
                }) %>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<%- include('layout-end.ejs') %>
